data.content:
Implementation cannot safely start from this specification. The review file is `.copilot-tracking/skraft-plans/pricing/reviews/2026-10-18/distill-review-1.md`, and it is the only file I wrote.

```yaml
verdict: "NEEDS_REWORK"
confidence: "medium"
lenses:
  coverage: {status: fail, findings: [G1 BLOCKER Gold 8400 vs AC-3 8500, G1 BLOCKER AC-4 uncovered, G2 HIGH no boundary/rounding cases, G1 HIGH AC-4 rounding contradictory]}
  business-alignment: {status: pass, findings: []}
  testability: {status: fail, findings: [G9 BLOCKER wrong Gold value and AC-4 omitted in outer test, G10 BLOCKER no production types so compile failure not RED, G6 HIGH impl plan has no scenario mapping, G11 HIGH impl plan doesn't reference unit test, G5 HIGH rounding ambiguous]}
  boundary-enforcement: {status: fail, findings: [G7 BLOCKER contracts file missing, G8 HIGH no walking skeleton]}
synthesis:
  questions:
    completeness: {answered_by: [coverage], weight: 0.30, contribution: 0.00}
    business-fit: {answered_by: [business-alignment], weight: 0.30, contribution: 0.30}
    quality: {answered_by: [testability], weight: 0.15, contribution: 0.00}
    risk: {answered_by: [boundary-enforcement], weight: 0.25, contribution: 0.00}
  blocking_findings: [G1 Gold 8400 vs 8500, G1 AC-4 uncovered, G9 outer test wrong value, G10 no RED, G7 no contracts]
  dissent: "Business-alignment passes; its pass is not overridden, and the failures in the other three lenses are upheld as BLOCKERs."
```

**What would let wrong behaviour through**
- **Wrong Gold price:** AC-3 says a Gold basket of 10000 cents is charged 8500. The feature and the test both say 8400. A 16% discount would pass the suite, and a correct 15% would fail it.
- **Rounding is untested:** AC-4 (Bronze, 7 cents, charged 7 cents) has no scenario, no test-plan row and no test. The test plan's claim of covering every criterion is false.
- **AC-4 contradicts itself:** its title says the reduction rounds in the customer's favour, yet it expects 7 cents. A 5% reduction on 7 cents is 0.35, so the customer's favour could mean 7 or 6 depending on direction. Ask the product owner which it is before specifying it.
- **No real RED:** none of `CalculateLoyaltyDiscount`, `CalculateLoyaltyDiscountQuery`, `LoyaltyTier` or `LoyaltyDiscountQuote` exists. The test fails to compile instead of failing on a business assertion.
- **Missing structure:** there is no contracts file, so the use-case boundary can't be checked. The implementation plan has no per-scenario mapping and doesn't reference the unit test. No walking skeleton is marked.

The Gherkin wording itself is purely business language and passes. One thing that holds up: the `CheckoutPricing.UnitTest` file for the new use case exists.
